Step 1 — Check the database recovery state
Connect to the secondary replica, open a fresh SSMS query window in master, and run:
SELECT
    @@SERVERNAME AS ServerName,
    name AS DatabaseName,
    state_desc,
    recovery_model_desc
FROM sys.databases
WHERE name IN ('DB1', 'DB2');

Step 2 — Check AG synchronization status
On the same secondary:
SELECT
    DB_NAME(database_id) AS DatabaseName,
    database_state_desc,
    synchronization_state_desc,
    synchronization_health_desc,
    is_suspended,
    suspend_reason_desc,
    log_send_queue_size,
    redo_queue_size
FROM sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states
WHERE is_local = 1
AND DB_NAME(database_id) IN ('DB1', 'DB2');

tep 3 — Check SQL Server error logs
Because the problem persists after restart, this is particularly important.

Run on the secondary:
EXEC sys.xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'DB1';
EXEC sys.xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'DB2';
